<p>Iran said on April 5 that Iraqi vessels can transit the Strait of Hormuz despite restrictions Tehran has imposed in the waterway, with military spokesperson Ebrahim Zolfaghari describing Iraq as exempt and &quot;brotherly&quot; in remarks carried by state media. The announcement matters because the strait is a vital global oil route, and the move could let Iraqi cargoes pass through after weeks of disruption to energy flows. Tehran tied the exemption to Iraq&#x27;s &quot;struggle&quot; against the United States, the reports said, showing that Iran is using its shipping restrictions to send a political message as well as an economic one. For readers elsewhere, the practical effect is straightforward: one of the region&#x27;s largest oil exporters may have a clearer path to market again, offering some relief for oil flows even as broader tensions around the strait remain unresolved.</p><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Oil volumes: The exemption could free up as much as 3 million barrels a day of Iraqi oil cargoes, a significant amount for global energy trade if transits normalize.</li><li>Weeks disrupted: SCMP said the broader Iranian restrictions had choked global energy flows for weeks before Tehran carved Iraq out.</li><li>Official channel: Iran delivered the message in an Arabic-language video statement published by the state-run Islamic Republic News Agency, making clear it wanted the exemption heard across the Arab world.</li></ul><blockquote>Brotherly Iraq is exempt from any restrictions we have imposed on the Strait of Hormuz - Ebrahim Zolfaghari</blockquote><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Iranian military: Military spokesperson Ebrahim Zolfaghari presented Iraq&#x27;s exemption as a deliberate carveout from Iran&#x27;s shipping restrictions and framed it as solidarity with a neighboring country. (<a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/4/5/iran-says-iraqi-ships-can-pass-strait-hormuz-as-transits-tick-up'>Al Jazeera</a>)</li><li>Iraq: In the coverage, Iraq is the immediate beneficiary because its cargoes may now move through the strait, potentially restoring export capacity constrained by the restrictions. (<a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-says-iraqi-ships-are-allowed-to-use-strait-of-hormuz'>The Straits Times</a>)</li><li>Global energy market: The reports cast the exemption as a step that could ease pressure on energy flows by reopening a route for a substantial share of Iraqi crude exports. (<a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3349069/iran-says-brotherly-iraqs-ships-can-use-strait-hormuz'>South China Morning Post</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.aljazeera.com/economy/2026/4/5/iran-says-iraqi-ships-can-pass-strait-of-hormuz-as-transits-tick-up'>Iran says Iraqi ships can pass Strait of Hormuz as transits tick up</a> - aljazeera.com</li><li><a href='https://www.scmp.com/news/world/middle-east/article/3349069/iran-says-brotherly-iraqs-ships-can-use-strait-hormuz'>Iran says ‘brotherly’ Iraq’s ships can use Strait of Hormuz</a> - scmp.com</li><li><a href='https://www.straitstimes.com/world/middle-east/iran-says-iraqi-ships-are-allowed-to-use-strait-of-hormuz'>Iran says Iraqi ships are allowed to use Strait of Hormuz</a> - straitstimes.com</li><li><a href='https://www.manilatimes.net/2026/04/05/world/iran-says-iraq-shipping-free-to-cross-strait-of-hormuz/2314321'>Military command spokesperson Ebrahim Zolfaghari says that Iran will allow vessels and ships from Iraq to use the Strait of Hormuz.</a> - manilatimes.net</li></ul>